================================================================================
Algorithms and technical acknowledgements
================================================================================

The following entries acknowledge short algorithms and mathematical techniques
implemented in the project source. They are not separately bundled libraries,
and these acknowledgements do not alter the project's GNU GPL v3 licensing.

Björn Ottosson - Oklab

The linear-sRGB/Oklab conversion matrices are from "A perceptual color space
for image processing". The accompanying conversion code is offered by its
author in the public domain or, alternatively, under the MIT License.
https://bottosson.github.io/posts/oklab/

Krzysztof Narkowicz - ACES curve fit

The ACES (Narkowicz fit) tone-map option uses Narkowicz's compact curve fit
to the ACES RRT+ODT, not the ACES transform itself. The published curve code is
offered under CC0 or the MIT License.
https://knarkowicz.wordpress.com/2016/01/06/aces-filmic-tone-mapping-curve/

Jorge Jimenez - Interleaved Gradient Noise

The dithering noise constants follow "Next Generation Post Processing in Call
of Duty: Advanced Warfare", presented at SIGGRAPH 2014. What is taken is the
three published constants and the fract() form around them, in
shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag and shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp. The
presentation attaches no license statement to the technique.
https://www.advances.realtimerendering.com/s2014/index.html

John Hable - Uncharted 2 filmic curve

The Filmic tone-map option follows the curve presented in "Uncharted 2: HDR
Lighting" at GDC 2010 and restated on the author's blog. What is taken is the
rational form of the curve and its six published constants, in
shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag. Neither the talk nor the blog post attaches
a license statement to the curve.
https://gdcvault.com/play/1012459/Uncharted-2-HDR
https://filmicworlds.com/blog/filmic-tonemapping-operators/

Erik Reinhard, Michael Stark, Peter Shirley, and James Ferwerda

The extended Reinhard tone-map option follows "Photographic Tone Reproduction
for Digital Images", SIGGRAPH 2002.
https://doi.org/10.1145/566654.566575

Peter Markstein - FMA division correction

The correctly rounded double division built from a hoisted reciprocal, in
shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp and shdsrc/palette_sampling.glsl (included by
shdsrc/vk_iteration_palette.frag), is the
two-FMA residual-and-correction step Markstein published in "Computation of
elementary functions on the IBM RISC System/6000 processor", IBM Journal of
Research and Development 34(1), 1990, and restated with Alan H. Karp in
"High-Precision Division and Square Root", ACM TOMS 23(4), 1997. The papers
attach no license statement to the technique.
https://doi.org/10.1147/rd.341.0111
https://doi.org/10.1145/279232.279237

Chris Wellons - lowbias32

The pattern-noise integer hash uses lowbias32 from hash-prospector, distributed
under the Unlicense.
https://github.com/skeeto/hash-prospector

Iñigo Quilez - cosine palettes

Procedural palette presets use the cosine-palette technique described in
"Palettes".
https://iquilezles.org/articles/palettes/

Boost C++ Libraries - hash_combine

The one-line seed-mixing expressions in src/vulkan_helper/hash/BoostHasher.hpp
and extern/nlohmann/json.hpp
follows the boost::hash_combine form and is used under the Boost Software
License 1.0, whose text and the accompanying copyright notice are reproduced
in the section below.
https://www.boost.org/LICENSE_1_0.txt

Kalles Fraktaler format compatibility

The .kfr palette reader supports the format's "Colors: " field. The Kalles
Fraktaler and KFR names identify file-format compatibility and related palette
functionality only.

HDR and display transfer functions - IEC, ITU-R, SMPTE, and ARIB

The HDR video export implements published colorimetry and transfer functions
from openly documented standards. Only the parameters needed to implement the
functions are used; no standards text is reproduced, and none of these bodies
endorses, sponsors, or certifies this program.

  ITU-R BT.709 and BT.2020    Primaries behind the linear-light matrix in
                              shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag, and the
                              bt2020 / bt2020nc tags on the encoder.
  IEC 61966-2-1 (sRGB)        EOTF and its inverse, used to move between stored
                              code values and linear light in
                              shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag,
                              shdsrc/vk_bloom.frag,
                              shdsrc/vk_static_2_image.frag, and
                              src/rff2/video/TimelineEvaluator.cpp.
                              Linear RGB palette interpolation also uses this
                              transfer in src/rff2/attr/ShdPaletteAttribute.h,
                              shdsrc/vk_iteration_palette.frag and
                              shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp.
                              shdsrc/vk_smooth_present.frag decodes display
                              values for an sRGB swapchain attachment.
  SMPTE ST 2084 (PQ)          Inverse EOTF in vk_linear_interpolation.frag and
                              src/rff2/ui/VideoWindow.cpp. Also published as
                              ITU-R BT.2100.
  SMPTE ST 2086               Form of the master-display metadata the PQ export
                              passes to the encoder.
  ARIB STD-B67 (HLG)          OETF in the same two files, developed by the BBC
                              and NHK and also published as ITU-R BT.2100.

The "HDR10 (PQ)" option is named for the profile it targets - 10-bit BT.2020
with the ST 2084 transfer and static ST 2086 metadata. HDR10 is an open profile
with no certification mark; nothing here claims conformance testing.

