================================================================================
Studio GGX and clearcoat - published equation references
================================================================================

The GGX distribution follows Bruce Walter, Stephen Marschner, Hongsong Li and
Kenneth Torrance, "Microfacet Models for Refraction through Rough Surfaces"
(2007): https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~srm/publications/EGSR07-btdf.pdf

Correlated Smith visibility follows B. G. Smith, "Geometrical Shadowing of a
Random Rough Surface" (1967), and Eric Heitz, "Understanding the Masking-
Shadowing Function in Microfacet-Based BRDFs" (2014):
https://jcgt.org/published/0003/02/03/

The Fresnel approximation follows Christophe Schlick, "An Inexpensive BRDF
Model for Physically-Based Rendering" (1994).

The anisotropic GGX/Smith equations and dielectric layering were cross-checked
against Romain Guy and Mathias Agopian's Filament documentation, sections 4.4,
4.9 and 4.10 (Apache-2.0):
https://google.github.io/filament/main/filament.html
https://github.com/google/filament/blob/main/LICENSE

================================================================================
Lustre surface - mathematical references
================================================================================

The supplied documentation/Lustre_II_RFF_Super_CPP_Porting_Guide_EN.md, sections 4, 5.2,
5.7 and 18.2, identifies the biased log-height/Sobel/AO construction, numeric
normal-filter parameters, RGB wavelength samples and four material recipes.
The project owner confirmed on 2026-09-23 that the original HTML was AI-generated
at their request. The guide is a record of the supplied mathematical specification.

Sobel refers to the discrete gradient operator associated with Irwin Sobel and
Gary Feldman (1968). Historical account by Irwin Sobel (2014/2015):
https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281104656_An_Isotropic_3x3_Image_Gradient_Operator

Geometric specular antialiasing references Yusuke Tokuyoshi and Anton S.
Kaplanyan, "Improved Geometric Specular Antialiasing" (I3D 2019):
https://yusuketokuyoshi.com/papers/2019/ImprovedGeometricSpecularAA.pdf

Thin-film tint references Snell's law and optical path difference as described
by Samuel J. Ling, Jeff Sanny and William Moebs, OpenStax, University Physics
Volume 3, section 3.4 (textbook content CC BY-NC-SA 4.0):
https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/3-4-interference-in-thin-films
This is a scientific reference, not a bundled textbook or a code license:
the shaders independently evaluate refraction and optical path difference.
No OpenStax prose, figures or sample code are included in those implementations.
The three artistic wavelength samples come from the supplied Lustre guide.

The 2026-09-24 base-material correction initializes the existing cycle and gradient
inputs for ordered lighting; it adds no third-party formulas or constants.

